# THE PRIVATE-REPO ALLOWLIST — links a public visitor CANNOT open, on purpose.
#
# lychee reads this file from the working directory automatically; every line
# is a regex matched against the URL. The link check (.github/workflows/
# link-check.yml) probes ANONYMOUSLY, exactly as a logged-out visitor does, so
# a private or internal repo answers 404 and is indistinguishable from a repo
# that was deleted or renamed away. This list is what tells the two apart, and
# it is the whole cost of the anonymous probe: every private repo the fleet
# pages link to has to be written down here, by hand, once.
#
# WHY ANONYMOUS AND NOT AUTHENTICATED. An authenticated probe would need no
# list at all — it sees every repo in the org — but it goes green on links the
# page's actual audience cannot open, which is the failure this check exists to
# catch. The pages under profile/ and fleet/ are the org's PUBLIC landing page.
# What a token can reach is not the question being asked.
#
# WHAT THIS LIST CANNOT DO, stated plainly: an entry below suppresses the 404
# for that URL forever. If one of these repos is later DELETED or RENAMED, its
# link is dead and this file hides it — the anonymous probe cannot tell a
# deleted repo from a private one, because both answer 404. Nothing here closes
# that hole; only reading the list against the org's real repos does. The
# workflow does the two checks that ARE possible: it fails when an entry names
# a URL no page links to any more, and it warns when an entry's repo has gone
# public and should therefore be checked for real.
#
# LINE FORMAT IS ENFORCED, not conventional. Every non-comment line must be an
# anchored literal URL — `^`, the URL with its dots escaped, `$` — because the
# staleness guard reconstructs the plain URL from it to grep the pages. A
# cleverer regex is rejected by that guard rather than silently mis-parsed.
# Anchoring is also load-bearing on its own: unanchored, `mimir` would swallow
# `mimir-automations`, and `stark-2nd-brain-cli` its `-hub` sibling.
#
# Measured 2026-09-20 against `gh repo list 21StarkCom`, after STARK-8035
# merged and STARK-8036 added the six rows it was missing: these pages link to
# 63 org repos, all 63 exist, and 3 of them are public — `.github`, `bifrost`,
# `stark-skills`. The other 60 are below, and
# the two section counts underneath have to keep summing to that 60. There is
# no dead link on the pages as this lands, which is exactly the state the
# check is here to keep.
#
# KEEPING THIS LIST HONEST is two jobs, and the workflow only does one of them.
# It fails on an entry no page links to any more and warns on one whose repo
# has gone public. What it cannot do is notice a repo below being DELETED. That
# one is caught by re-deriving the list — link the pages' repo names against
# `gh repo list 21StarkCom` and look for a name the org does not have. That is
# the check that found `stark-clickup`.

# ── PRIVATE (58) — invisible to everyone outside the org.
